# PRD — theprawnstatus

**Repo:** `hongyime/theprawnstatus` (public)
**One-liner:** One board showing whether all 14 deployments are up, and whether all 69 repos still meet the SHELL standard.
**Status:** Approved — ready to build
**Date:** 2026-08-09 (rev 3)

**Changelog from rev 2:**
- SHELL is no longer a hard blocker. `STANDARD.md` exists; the health half is unblocked pending a token and a config file.
- The health half is reframed. `hongyime/sourcerepo` already *pushes* configuration to all 69 repos weekly but never *reports* what is broken. theprawnstatus is that missing report, not a generic linter.
- Added a hard privacy boundary (§4) — this repo never handles identity data.
- UptimeRobot's current free tier verified; kill criteria in §13 tightened rather than softened.

---

## 1. Summary

The Prawn Organisation (`hongyime`) has 14 live Vercel apps on `*.hong-yi.me` and ~69 repos. There is currently no way to know that `smuseats.hong-yi.me` has been 502-ing for three weeks, or that six repos have no LICENSE.

theprawnstatus polls every deployment on a schedule, records status and latency, and renders a rolling 90-day uptime board. It then does the same for repository *health*: reading the GitHub API for every org repo and checking it against the standard SHELL defines.

Two signals, one page: **is it running** and **is it up to standard**.

---

## 2. Why this, why you

- It is the one project you would look at every day, which makes it the one most likely to actually get finished.
- **It closes a named gap in SHELL.** `sourcerepo` runs a weekly cross-repo sync that pushes settings, security workflows, config files, and community health files. It is write-only. Nothing anywhere reports whether the push landed, whether a repo drifted back, or whether the sync itself is misconfigured. theprawnstatus is the read side of that loop.
- It requires no third-party service, no database, no auth. The whole thing is a cron job and a static page.
- `theprawnprojects` already reads the Vercel API, so deployment discovery partly exists.
- It respects the SPAWN inference budget: zero LLM calls at runtime. Every check is deterministic.

### The relationship to SHELL, precisely

| Concern | Owner |
|---|---|
| Defining "production ready" | SHELL — `STANDARD.md` |
| Enforcing settings across repos | `sourcerepo` — weekly sync |
| Scanning for personal identifiers | SHELL — `scan_identity.py`, runs with `$SHELL_IDENTITY` |
| **Reporting what is currently non-compliant** | **theprawnstatus** |
| Fixing non-compliance | SHELL / `sourcerepo` — never theprawnstatus |

theprawnstatus is strictly read-only against the rest of the estate. It has no write access to any repo but its own `data` branch.

### First test case

`sourcerepo`'s `sync-repo-settings.yml` currently hardcodes `description: "Give me 1 ⭐ if it's cool."` and overwrites all 69 repos every Monday. The board should therefore show `description_is_default` at 69/69 failing every Tuesday, sawtoothing back down as the week progresses only if something else corrects it. The day that pattern disappears is the day SHELL gap #1 is genuinely fixed. If the board *doesn't* show this, the health checker is wrong — this is a known-answer test.

